# 🔐 Reverse IP Lookup: Shared Hosting Security Assessment

## Overview

During Day 5 of my security assessment journey, I discovered that even if a website appears secure, the server behind it may harbor vulnerabilities through co-hosted websites.

## The Problem

Shared hosting environments create a dangerous scenario:
- Multiple websites on a single IP address
- One vulnerable site can compromise the entire server
- Attackers can pivot from weak sites to high-value targets
- Limited isolation between hosted applications

## Reverse IP Lookup Methodology

### Step 1: Identify Target IP Address

```bash
# Get IP address of target
ping target.com

# Using nslookup
nslookup target.com

# Using dig
dig +short target.com
```

### Step 2: Perform Reverse IP Lookup

```bash
# Using Shodan CLI
shodan host 192.168.1.1

# Using theHarvester
theHarvester -d target.com -b all

# Web-based tools
# - viewdns.info/reverseip
# - bing.com (ip:192.168.1.1)
# - securitytrails.com
```

### Step 3: Analyze Co-hosted Websites

```bash
# Check technologies on discovered sites
whatweb -i domains.txt

# Screenshot all domains
gowitness file -f domains.txt

# Check for common vulnerabilities
nuclei -l domains.txt -t cves/
```

### Step 4: Identify Weak Links

```bash
# Scan for outdated CMS
wpscan --url http://weaksite.com

# Check for exposed admin panels
gobuster dir -u http://weaksite.com -w admin-panels.txt
```

